// Bounded primary-law Accuracy Batch 3 — case-specific legal notes for 2024 and 2025 sources.
// Explanations are additive and do not certify subsequent case treatment or external academic review.
(function(){'use strict';
const additions=[["L3-3-3","CORNER POST TIMING CHECK (July 1, 2024). A trade association challenges the emergency workplace rule after a newly formed member suffers its first compliance injury years after promulgation. Under Corner Post, for APA claims governed by 28 U.S.C. §2401(a), the six-year clock starts when that plaintiff first has a complete claim—injury caused by final agency action—not automatically when the regulation was published. This is not an exemption from a more specific statutory filing deadline, and it does not cure a missing final action, standing, ripeness or failure to satisfy a procedural prerequisite. AGENCY: a special statute governing the industry imposes a shorter review deadline. CHALLENGER: the general six-year period controls and began at its later injury. Write an effective-date, finality, injury and deadline table and an opposing timeliness brief. Check the source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1008; later precedent and statute-specific review remain pending."],["L3-3-6","ENFORCEMENT DISCRETION VERSUS CHALLENGE TIMELINESS. Heckler v. Chaney's presumption regarding an agency decision not to enforce is analytically separate from judicial review of a binding industry-wide directive and the statute of limitations. Under Corner Post, an APA action subject to §2401(a) accrues upon the particular plaintiff's injury from final agency action, not solely the action's original issuance. Do not assert that every agency refusal is judicially reviewable or that a recent injury automatically supplies reviewability for discretionary nonenforcement. AGENCY: the complaint targets discretionary case selection. RESPONDENT: a legally binding directive has independent final consequences. Draft separate reviewability and limitations charts, identify the relevant enabling act's special deadline if any, and propose conditional rulings. Primary source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1008. Full later-treatment review pending."],["L3-3-8","FINAL ACTION AND LATE-INJURED CHALLENGER. The rulemaking record challenge asks whether the agency seriously considered alternatives and explained its policy change under the governing review standard; that merits question is distinct from when a particular plaintiff's APA claim accrues. Corner Post held the general §2401(a) six-year period starts when the plaintiff is injured by final agency action, not automatically at promulgation. GOVERNMENT: the plaintiff may face a specific direct-review deadline, and independent standing/finality objections may still apply. PETITIONER: a new operator first injured by the rule may bring a timely challenge to a much older rule under the default period. Prepare a plaintiff-specific harm timeline, limitations analysis, merits record table and a changed-facts disposition for a previously existing injured operator. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1008. No certified later review."],["L3-3-9","APA REMEDY AND TIMELINESS SEPARATION. In the manufacturer's successful rule challenge, distinguish the question whether the APA action was timely from the separate authority for prospective relief, remand, vacatur, refunds or monetary recovery. Corner Post concerns the accrual of an APA claim under §2401(a) when the plaintiff is first injured by final agency action; it does not itself authorize repayment of previously paid charges, waive every sovereign immunity issue or prescribe a universal remedial form. COMPANY: present evidence of the first injury and a statutory source of requested refund authority. AGENCY: challenge jurisdiction, limitation under a specific statute and any unsupported retroactive financial remedy. Prepare separate liability, accrual and relief matrices. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1008; independently verify applicable refund law."],["L4-5-5","RULEMAKING COMMENT AND CORNER POST REVIEW TIMELINE. A trade group submits scientific comments and years later a new member company first incurs costs under the finalized rule. Do not assume the APA default limitations period necessarily expired six years after promulgation. Corner Post holds an APA claim under §2401(a) accrues upon that particular plaintiff's injury by final agency action, subject to any more specific judicial-review provision. Distinguish agency rule publication, comment deadlines, effective date, individual injury, judicial-review filing periods, and exhaustion/preservation requirements where controlling law demands them. The government may rely on a specific industry statute; the new business asserts the general period. Deliver a 12-event litigation calendar, record index and opposed timeliness memorandum. Primary Court opinion: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1008. Review later cases and forum separately."],["L4-5-10","REGULATORY PRACTICAL FINAL: LIMITATIONS IS NOT THE MERITS. The transportation operator is served with an agency subpoena and challenges an older final rule that now affects its operations. Require the candidate to distinguish the subpoena's own enforcement authority from a direct challenge to the underlying rule, whether a final agency action exists, the operator's first cognizable injury and the operative limitations provision. Corner Post construed §2401(a) to begin upon individual injury from final agency action, rather than a fixed six-year clock from promulgation; special statutes may alter the period. AGENCY: the hearing may depend on a different and shorter statute. OPERATOR: procedural and merits challenges must be preserved distinctly. Submit a dated procedural route map and two alternative orders; retain the existing final's original grading key. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1008. Unverified downstream holdings."],["L3-2-6","MACQUARIE SECURITIES DISCLOSURE DISTINCTION (2024). The executives' known production problem must be mapped against the precise public investor statements and the current SEC disclosure duty. Macquarie Infrastructure Corp. v. Moab Partners held that a pure omission, including failure to make an Item 303 disclosure alone, does not establish a private Rule 10b-5(b) action without making an affirmative statement misleading. It did not excuse compliance with SEC filing requirements or decide all other Rule 10b-5 provisions. INVESTORS: identify the actual favorable production statement whose missing qualifier made it a half-truth, then prove all other required claim elements. ISSUER: distinguish silence from a misleading affirmative statement and contest materiality and scienter on the actual record. Submit an omitted-fact versus statement-made matrix and revised complaint/defense. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1165. Subsequent treatment pending."],["L4-3-10","MERGER FINAL: SEC OMISSIONS AND ACTUAL STATEMENTS. The acquisition dossier's unresolved data incident must not automatically be treated as private Rule 10b-5(b) fraud merely because a regulatory disclosure might have been due. Under Macquarie, pure silence, without an affirmative statement thereby rendered misleading, is not actionable under Rule 10b-5(b); an Item 303 disclosure obligation alone does not supply that missing element. Separate contractual representations and closing conditions from securities fraud, and separately identify any SEC filing or enforcement issue. BUYER: a signed representation may be inaccurate even if no securities half-truth occurred. SELLER: an undisclosed fact cannot by itself prove a private statement-based antifraud claim. Draft a statement-by-statement disclosure chart, alternative remedies and revised board recommendation. Primary opinion: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1165; later and transaction-specific law pending."],["L5-3-4","ADVANCED SECURITIES AND SALE PROCESS. The public-company sale creates potential duties under both Delaware entity law and federal securities regulations. Macquarie (2024) requires an affirmative misleading statement for a private Rule 10b-5(b) omission theory; failure to disclose a required Item 303 topic without a misleading affirmative representation is a pure omission and cannot independently sustain that particular private claim. Distinguish the SEC's regulatory authority, other statutory claims, and contract- or fiduciary-law disclosures. CLAIMANT: pinpoint which board or issuer statement became a half-truth and its materiality. DEFENSE: absence of the identified statement and disputed scienter. Submit a source-to-claim table, competing written briefs and two changed-fact versions showing silence versus optimistic published guidance. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1165. No blanket absolution for nondisclosure."],["L3-2-2","DELAWARE DGCL §144 2025 SOURCE UPDATE. The directors' approval of a deal involving a sibling's valuation raises independence and informed-process questions, but the operative statute depends on the corporation's actual state of incorporation and transaction-related law. Delaware's March 25, 2025 amendment to §144 establishes distinct pathways for interested director/officer transactions under subsection (a), non-going-private controller transactions under (b), and going-private controller transactions under (c). Do not automatically treat all three as subject to identical committee and minority-vote requirements. For a controller going-private transaction, §144(c)(1) refers to BOTH the committee and disinterested-stockholder procedures, or fairness under (c)(2). The bill includes applicability exceptions for proceedings or inspection demands made on or before Feb. 17, 2025. Draft a transaction-date and director-independence ledger; compare a director conflict with true controller going-private. Enacted source: https://legis.delaware.gov/SessionLaws/Chapter?id=42168 and current code https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc04/. Review modern judicial construction separately."],["L3-2-3","DEMAND AND DGCL §144: TWO DISTINCT QUESTIONS. A shareholder alleging favorable treatment of an affiliated supplier must distinguish derivative standing, the governing demand requirement, and the standard for reviewing the underlying conflicted deal. Federal Rule 23.1 regulates pleading and procedure in federal court but does not replace the state substantive demand-futility framework. Delaware's 2025 §144 amendments affect statutory fiduciary protections for specified director/controller transactions and should not be invoked as an automatic exception to derivative-demand rules or as an all-purpose answer to a pleaded oversight failure. SHAREHOLDER: itemize directors, ties and actual benefits; BOARD: evaluate disinterested approval and the effective-date regime. Deliver a seat-by-seat demand table and independent transaction safe-harbor chart. Official law: https://legis.delaware.gov/SessionLaws/Chapter?id=42168. Applicable derivative precedent and judicial application pending."],["L4-3-1","M&A DILIGENCE — DELAWARE STATUTORY CONFLICTS. A competing-business acquisition turns on actual change-of-control language and closing conditions, not merely general fiduciary labels. If a Delaware corporation has an interested director, the revised 2025 §144(a) may provide particular statutory processes or a fairness pathway; if a controlling stockholder receives a nonshared benefit, subsection (b) or (c) may apply instead. An ordinary asset purchase or contract dispute may not fit controller definitions at all. Distinguish (1) buyer's counterparty contract consent, (2) approval by disinterested directors or stockholders and verified disclosures, (3) statutory effective-date exceptions, and (4) antitrust filing only if thresholds actually apply. Create separate approval and closing-condition checklists, adverse positions and a proposed deal timetable. Primary source: https://legis.delaware.gov/SessionLaws/Chapter?id=42168; jurisdiction and later treatment pending."],["L4-3-6","2025 §144 TRANSACTION-PROCESS CORRECTION. The director who owns shares of the target may be materially interested; identify the director's specific financial stake and what the board and committee knew. Under amended DGCL §144(a), qualifying disclosed, disinterested director/committee approval, informed uncoerced disinterested-stockholder approval, or fairness may be relevant for covered non-controller conflicts. Distinguish a genuine controller transaction under §144(b), including an informed committee OR disinterested stockholder pathway (or fairness), from a going-private controller deal under §144(c), which generally requires both specified approval pathways or fairness. The statutory conditions, independent committee composition, and March 25, 2025 applicability/Feb. 17 grandfathering exception must be checked. BOARD: argue qualified process; CHALLENGER: show defective independence or inadequate disclosure. Submit an operative-law matrix, committee charter and reasoned alternate disposition. Official source: https://legis.delaware.gov/SessionLaws/Chapter?id=42168."],["L5-3-1","CAREMARK VERSUS CONFLICTED TRANSACTION STATUTES. A board ignoring sanctions and product safety reports may raise a Caremark-style oversight claim based on failure of monitoring systems or a conscious failure to respond to red flags; it is not transformed into a controller conflict safe-harbor problem merely because the board also approved an affiliated acquisition. Analyze the two causes separately and identify actual knowing inaction and claimed loss for oversight. If Delaware §144 applies to the related-party transaction, the 2025 amendments distinguish ordinary director interests and controller transactions, with separate statutory protections and exceptions. A statutory process does not categorically eliminate every other corporate-law, statutory authorization or aiding-and-abetting claim. Prepare two duty and remedy charts with transaction dates and adverse conclusions. Official code: https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc04/; specific current judicial readings remain pending."],["L5-3-4","DELAWARE GOING-PRIVATE PROCESS CROSSCHECK. In the public-company sale, the controller's side agreement may create a nonshared benefit, but do not call every controller deal 'going private.' Verify the §144(e)(6) definition and the actual share treatment. For covered going-private controller transactions, amended §144(c) provides protection if qualifying committee AND disinterested stockholder paths are followed, or if the transaction is fair; a non-going-private controller transaction is treated separately under §144(b), and ordinary conflicts under (a). The March 25, 2025 act contains express transition rules, including exception for specified proceedings and inspection demands by February 17, 2025. Alter the scenario: a minority holder retains publicly traded shares, versus all minority shares cashed out. Produce a four-part timeline, independent committee and vote matrix, strongest claimant and board arguments, and the applicable section of enacted text: https://legis.delaware.gov/SessionLaws/Chapter?id=42168."],["L5-3-13","CONTROLLER DEAL SAFE-HARBOR CHECK — AMENDED §144. The existing case asks whether a cash-out approval was structured with genuine independent bargaining and disinterested shareholder protection. Delaware's 2025 law distinguishes the §144(b) non-going-private controller category from the §144(c) going-private category: for the latter, the prescribed committee AND shareholder processes must both be satisfied, or the transaction must be fair, to qualify for that protection. Confirm the controller definition, actual minority share acquisition, committee authority and composition, materially complete disclosures, and transaction date. Do not describe §144 protection as universal permission for any breach, or assume an earlier MFW case applies without checking the operative statute and transition. Deliver a dated approval map, alternative outcomes and source verification. Official current statute: https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc04/."]];
const lessons=new Map([...(window.LAW_LEVEL3||[]).flatMap(c=>c.lessons||[]),...(window.LAW_LEVEL4||[]).flatMap(c=>c.lessons||[]),...(window.LAW_LEVEL5||[]).flatMap(c=>c.lessons||[])].map(l=>[l.id,l]));
if(lessons.size!==310)throw Error('All Level 3-5 course data must load first');
for(const [id,note] of additions){const lesson=lessons.get(id);if(!lesson||typeof lesson.reading!=='string')throw Error('Unknown source-review lesson '+id);if(!lesson.reading.includes(note))lesson.reading+='\n\nDATED PRIMARY-LAW RESEARCH NOTE (2026-11-13): '+note;}
window.LAW_CROSSLEVEL_PRIMARY_SOURCE_BATCH3_IDS=additions.map(([id])=>id);
})();